        fp: "A master commercial stone mason entered into a written contract with an art museum to construct a decorative granite fountain in the museum's courtyard for $80,000, with work to be completed by August 1. Under the agreement, the museum was to make monthly progress payments based on percentage of completion. On July 1, after the mason had completed 75% of the fountain and had received $60,000 in progress payments, the mason suffered an acute cardiac episode requiring immediate double-bypass heart surgery. The mason's physician ordered him to cease all heavy physical labor for a minimum of nine months. The mason telephoned the museum director, explained his medical incapacitation, and stated that he would be physically unable to complete the fountain. The museum solicited bids from three licensed, reputable stone masons to finish the remaining 25% of the granite work; the lowest competitive bid was $28,000. The museum hired the replacement mason, who completed the fountain on August 1 for $28,000. The original mason sued the museum in quantum meruit, seeking $10,000, arguing that the reasonable market value of his completed 75% labor and stone materials was $70,000, whereas he had been paid only $60,000.",
