        ans: 1,
        exp: "Rule: While the traditional common law pre-existing duty rule barred contract modifications without fresh consideration, the modern prevailing rule (Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 89(a)) provides an exception: a promise modifying a duty under a contract not fully performed on either side is binding if the modification is fair and equitable in view of circumstances not anticipated by the parties when the contract was made. Encountering massive, unmapped 19th-century subterranean concrete foundations that require heavy hydraulic breaker machinery and substantially increase performance costs is an unforeseen, unanticipated circumstance. Because the modification was in writing, entered into voluntarily before performance was completed, and addressed unanticipated substantial physical difficulties, it is legally binding without fresh consideration (Option B). Option A is incorrect because Restatement § 89 provides a recognized exception to the pre-existing duty rule for unanticipated circumstances. Option C is incorrect because requesting a price adjustment to cover unforeseen buried obstacles does not constitute unlawful economic duress. Option D is incorrect because excavation is a service contract governed by common law, not UCC Article 2."
